  "title": "Changing variables",
  "summary": "Update a value using its old value, the += shorthand, swapping, and good names.",
  "language": "python",
  "minutes": 20,
  "visual": "memory",
  "visuals": [
    "memory",
    "history"
  ],
  "intro": {
    "text": "`=` always works **right side first**. Python works out the value on the right using the values names have right now, then puts the result in the name on the left. So `score = score + 10` is fine: take the old score, add 10, store the new score.\n\nThis is so common that Python has a shorthand: `score += 10` means the same. There is also `-=`, `*=`, `/=`, `//=` and `%=`.\n\nTo swap two values you need a spare name to hold one of them for a moment, or one of the values gets lost.\n\nNames may use letters, digits and `_`, must not start with a digit, and cannot be words Python already uses, like `class` or `for`. Good names say what they hold: `total_price` beats `tp`.",
    "analogy": "Think of a scoreboard. To add 10 points, you read the number that is up there now, add 10 in your head, then wipe the board and write the new number. Reading always happens before writing."
  },
